const moziSections: ArticleSection[] = [
  {id: 'orientation', title: 'A master known through a movement', paragraphs: [
    p('mozi-orientation-1', 'Mozi, or Master Mo, names both an elusive fifth-century BCE teacher and the point of origin claimed by a durable Warring States movement. Early testimony supplies little dependable biography. He probably flourished in the middle or later fifth century BCE, after Confucius, but familiar exact dates and stories about his birthplace, social status, or government service are reconstructions rather than documentary facts. The safest historical portrait begins with a reforming teacher whose followers became prominent enough that later writers paired Mohists with Confucians as major rivals.', [c('mozi-sep', 'section', '1. Mozi and the Mohists'), c('mozi-iep', 'section', '1. Historical Background')]),
    p('mozi-orientation-2', 'Those followers were more than readers of one book. Sources depict an organized community that trained advocates, recommended members for office, and cultivated expertise in defending threatened cities. Its members transmitted ten characteristic policies concerning worthy appointment, political conformity, inclusive concern, aggression, expenditure, funerals, music, Heaven, ghosts, and fatalism. The evidence does not justify attributing every surviving formulation or institutional practice directly to Mozi. This article therefore alternates deliberately between “Mozi” for the remembered founder and “the Mohists” or “the Mozi” for claims preserved by the movement and anthology.', [c('mozi-sep', 'section', '1–2'), c('mozi-iep', 'section', '1–3')]),
  ]},
  {id: 'corpus', title: 'The layered Mozi corpus', paragraphs: [
    p('mozi-corpus-1', 'The received Mozi is not a single authored treatise. It is an anthology of separate scroll-length writings assembled and edited over time; eighteen of the seventy-one books listed in an early catalogue are lost. Its major blocks differ in date, voice, and purpose: introductory miscellanies, the doctrinal triads, dialectical or “later Mohist” chapters, dialogues and anecdotes, and technical chapters on defensive warfare. No surviving portion announces itself as written by Mo Di. Repetition can preserve teaching and oral performance, but it cannot by itself establish the founder’s wording.', [c('mozi-sep', 'section', 'Supplement: Texts and Authorship'), c('mozi-iep', 'section', '2. The Core Chapters of the Mozi')]),
    p('mozi-corpus-2', 'The ten doctrines appear in sets conventionally called triads, usually three essays on one program. Variations among them may reflect different lineages, dates, audiences, or editorial histories, and several members of the sets are missing. A sound interpretation can identify recurring Mohist commitments without flattening the versions into a timeless system. The dialogues likewise offer philosophically useful portraits but not stenographic records. Even the book title should not trick readers into imagining one composition comparable to a modern monograph; it names the collected literature of a school anchored in the authority of Master Mo.', [c('mozi-sep', 'section', '2. The Ten Mohist Doctrines; Supplement: Texts and Authorship'), c('mozi-iep', 'section', '2. The Core Chapters of the Mozi')]),
  ], relatedWorkTitles: ['Mozi']},
  {id: 'benefit-standards', title: 'Benefit and public standards', paragraphs: [
    p('mozi-benefit-1', 'Mohist arguments judge teachings and practices by whether they promote benefit and remove harm for the world. The goods repeatedly foregrounded are concrete: material sufficiency, an adequate population, and social order. This makes “consequentialist” a useful comparison, because outcomes for everyone matter more than inherited prestige or ritual beauty. It does not make the Mohists straightforward hedonistic or preference utilitarians. Benefit is plural and politically framed, order is itself a major good, and Heaven’s morally charged will participates in justification rather than merely adding supernatural decoration to a secular maximizing rule.', [c('mozi-sep', 'section', '3. The Search for Objective Standards; 7. Ethical Theory'), c('mozi-ethics-sep', 'section', '3.1 A consequentialist ethic, but what kind?'), c('mozi-johnston', 'chapter', '16. Inclusive Care III; 25. Economy in Expenditures II')]),
    p('mozi-benefit-2', 'The Mohists call reliable models or standards fa. A craft analogy is central: just as a wheelwright or carpenter uses a compass, square, or measured model rather than private impulse, moral and political judgment should appeal to publicly teachable patterns. The famous threefold test asks about a proposal’s basis, evidence, and practical application, though the triads formulate and use these checks in ways that raise questions about circularity. Past sage practice, ordinary testimony, and beneficial results can support judgment, but disagreement remains over how Heaven, historical examples, and consequences jointly ground the standards.', [c('mozi-sep', 'section', '3. The Search for Objective Standards; 4. Epistemology'), c('mozi-iep', 'section', '5. Moral Epistemology'), c('mozi-johnston', 'chapter', '4. Models and Standards; 35–37. Against Fatalism')]),
  ]},
  {id: 'impartial-concern', title: 'Inclusive or impartial concern', paragraphs: [
    p('mozi-care-1', 'Jian ai is often translated as “universal love,” but “inclusive care” or “impartial concern” better signals the practical demand. The core diagnosis is that agents who treat only their own person, family, or state as worthy of protection generate theft, injury, domination, and aggressive war. Mohist reform asks people and rulers to treat others’ welfare as reason-giving rather than as negligible merely because it belongs to outsiders. The target is exclusive partiality; the remedy includes reciprocal aid, material support, and political conduct, not an instruction to manufacture identical feelings toward every stranger.', [c('mozi-sep', 'section', '7. Ethical Theory — Inclusive Care'), c('mozi-iep', 'section', '7. Impartial Concern'), c('mozi-johnston', 'chapter', '14–16. Inclusive Care')]),
    p('mozi-care-2', 'Whether Mohist concern requires equal allocation in every relationship is disputed. Some texts allow agents to fulfill family responsibilities while insisting that they not injure or disregard other families; some arguments promise that caring for others will secure care for one’s own. Confucian critics understood the doctrine as erasing graded family affection, while modern interpreters distinguish impartial moral consideration from uniform action or emotion. It is therefore inaccurate either to reduce jian ai to ordinary benevolence or to declare without qualification that Mozi demanded exactly equal love. The secure point is its challenge to morally privileged exclusion.', [c('mozi-ethics-sep', 'section', '3.2 Impartiality and the structure of Mohist concern'), c('mozi-sep', 'section', '7. Ethical Theory — Inclusive Care; Practical Objections'), c('mozi-iep', 'section', '7. Impartial Concern')]),
  ], relatedBranchIds: ['mohism', 'ethics']},
  {id: 'war', title: 'Against aggression, not every use of force', paragraphs: [
    p('mozi-war-1', 'The anti-aggression chapters expose a moral inconsistency: rulers condemn theft and murder among individuals while praising conquest that multiplies the same injuries. War destroys lives, crops, tools, and political security while imposing labor and expense on ordinary households. Mohist condemnation is therefore a test case for impartial judgment across differences of scale and status. Calling the view simply “pacifism,” however, goes too far. The texts condemn offensive aggression while accepting punishment, political coercion, and armed defense under specified descriptions of justice and benefit.', [c('mozi-johnston', 'chapter', '17–19. Against Offensive Warfare'), c('mozi-sep', 'section', '6. Political Theory; 7. Ethical Theory'), c('mozi-iep', 'section', '11. Just War')]),
    p('mozi-war-2', 'The final corpus block preserves detailed counter-siege techniques, apparently produced by communities prepared to aid smaller states. That practical expertise joins argument and institution: Mohists did not merely lament violence but sought to make conquest harder. Yet the military chapters are late, damaged, and distinct from the doctrinal triads, so their every device cannot be assigned to the founder. The broader political tension remains important. An ethic opposing interstate predation can coexist here with centralized hierarchy and severe enforcement, reminding readers that impartial benefit does not automatically yield modern liberal or anti-authoritarian politics.', [c('mozi-sep', 'section', 'Supplement: Texts and Authorship; 6. Political Theory'), c('mozi-iep', 'section', '1. Historical Background; 11. Just War'), c('mozi-war-ghosts', 'page', '343–363')]),
  ]},
  {id: 'heaven-ghosts', title: 'Heaven, ghosts, and moral enforcement', paragraphs: [
    p('mozi-heaven-1', 'Heaven is not a dispensable metaphor in the core Mohist program. The Heaven’s Intention chapters describe a morally purposive authority that desires righteousness and benefit, condemns aggression, and supplies an impartial standard above rulers. This religious framework supports the universality of Mohist norms and rebukes political rank: a powerful sovereign cannot make injury right by decree. Interpretive debates ask whether consequences or Heaven are foundational, but a historically responsible account does not secularize the texts in advance. Benefit, exemplars, and Heaven operate together even when their exact justificatory order is difficult to settle.', [c('mozi-johnston', 'chapter', '26–28. Heaven’s Intention'), c('mozi-sep', 'section', '7. Ethical Theory — Justification and the Role of Heaven; 8. Religion'), c('mozi-iep', 'section', '6. The Foundations of Mohist Morality; 12. Heaven and Spirits')]),
    p('mozi-heaven-2', 'The ghosts chapters defend the capacity of ghosts and spirits to reward the worthy and punish wrongdoing. Their arguments appeal to testimony and historical reports, but also to the social consequences of widespread belief: officials and subjects who expect unseen accountability have reasons not to abuse power in secret. It remains controversial whether this is cynical promotion of a useful belief regardless of truth. The texts present evidence for the spirits and treat their reality seriously, while emphasizing political benefit. Mozi should therefore be neither modernized into a religious skeptic nor portrayed as accepting every inherited ritual practice.', [c('mozi-johnston', 'chapter', '31. Elucidating Ghosts III'), c('mozi-war-ghosts', 'page', '343–363'), c('mozi-sep', 'section', '8. Religion'), c('mozi-iep', 'section', '12. Heaven and Spirits')]),
  ], relatedBranchIds: ['philosophy-of-religion']},
  {id: 'frugality-fatalism', title: 'Frugality, ritual criticism, and agency', paragraphs: [
    p('mozi-frugality-1', 'Mohist attacks on elaborate funerals, prolonged mourning, court music, and luxury follow the benefit standard. Resources and labor diverted into elite display cannot feed, clothe, shelter, or protect the population; extended mourning can also interrupt production and public duty. The position is not a general hatred of art derived from a theory that music has no value whatsoever. It is a political priority under scarcity and disorder, expressed with a severity that critics reasonably challenge. The question is comparative: what practices deserve public support when basic welfare and peace remain unsecured?', [c('mozi-johnston', 'chapter', '20–21. Economy in Funerals; 32. Condemning Music I'), c('mozi-sep', 'section', '2. The Ten Mohist Doctrines; 7. Ethical Theory'), c('mozi-iep', 'section', '10. Frugality')]),
    p('mozi-frugality-2', 'Against Fatalism rejects doctrines that make wealth, office, order, or early death fixed by ming, often translated as fate or destiny. Mohists argue that fatalism discourages effort and excuses rulers from effective policy, conflicting with evidence that disciplined action changes outcomes. This emphasis on agency fits their educational and institutional confidence: incentives, standards, appointments, and persuasion can redirect conduct. It does not imply total individual control over fortune. Their target is a socially disabling thesis, and their alternative is collective practical efficacy under conditions, not the modern claim that every person is solely responsible for whatever happens.', [c('mozi-johnston', 'chapter', '35–37. Against Fatalism'), c('mozi-sep', 'section', '2. The Ten Mohist Doctrines; 4. Epistemology'), c('mozi-iep', 'section', '3. The Ten Core Theses of Mohism')]),
  ]},
  {id: 'government', title: 'Merit and conformity in Mohist government', paragraphs: [
    p('mozi-government-1', '“Elevating the worthy” attacks hereditary and status-bound appointment by urging rulers to employ capable, morally committed people. Reward and office should track performance rather than noble birth, a striking reform within aristocratic politics. Yet Mohist merit is not neutral expertise: the worthy advance the movement’s conception of righteousness and benefit. The proposal seeks an administration able to secure material welfare, defense, and order. Calling it modern democratic equality would conceal both its monarchical setting and its confidence that correctly chosen superiors can identify and transmit common standards.', [c('mozi-johnston', 'chapter', '8–10. Elevating the Worthy'), c('mozi-sep', 'section', '6. Political Theory — Elevating the Worthy'), c('mozi-iep', 'section', '9. Government')]),
    p('mozi-government-2', '“Conforming upward” explains disorder as conflicting judgments and proposes a hierarchy in which subordinates report, learn, and align judgments through successive superiors up to the ruler and Heaven. The scheme aims to make public norms consistent and information travel through government, but it also risks suppressing dissent and placing immense trust in the apex. Mohist texts retain space for remonstrance and for judging rulers against Heaven rather than mere command, yet they do not solve every authoritarian danger. Inclusive concern and objective standards must therefore be studied alongside, not substituted for, political centralization.', [c('mozi-johnston', 'chapter', '11–13. Conforming Upward'), c('mozi-sep', 'section', '6. Political Theory — Critical Assessment'), c('mozi-iep', 'section', '9. Government')]),
  ], relatedBranchIds: ['political-philosophy']},
  {id: 'later-mohists', title: 'Later Mohist language, knowledge, and argument', paragraphs: [
    p('mozi-later-1', 'The Canons, Explanations, Greater Selection, Lesser Selection, and related texts belong to anonymous later Mohists, probably compiled from the late fourth to mid-third century BCE. They extend Mohist concerns into semantics, knowledge, analogy, ontology, geometry, mechanics, and optics. Because these chapters are terse and textually corrupt, modern reconstructions differ sharply. Their sophistication is crucial evidence against the old stereotype that early Chinese thought lacked sustained analysis, but admiration should not encourage attribution to Mozi himself or turn every technical distinction into an exact counterpart of modern formal logic.', [c('mozi-canons-sep', 'section', '1. Background and Overview; 2. The Texts'), c('mozi-sep', 'section', 'Supplement: Texts and Authorship; 5. Logic and Argumentation')]),
    p('mozi-later-2', 'Later Mohist inquiry treats names as presented for kinds of things, knowledge through abilities to distinguish reliably, and disputation through patterns of comparison and extension. The Lesser Selection catalogs methods that can support or mislead inference, especially when expressions change their role in complex contexts. These are logic-like achievements without a developed theory of valid argument form identical to Aristotelian syllogistic or modern symbolic logic. The right comparison asks what problems the texts solve in their own vocabulary—same and different, kind, reason, possibility, naming—before translating them into a later discipline.', [c('mozi-canons-sep', 'section', '4. Philosophy of Language; 5. Epistemology; 7. Argumentation and Logic'), c('mozi-johnston', 'chapter', '40–45. Canons and Explanations; Greater and Lesser Selection')]),
  ], relatedBranchIds: ['logic', 'epistemology', 'philosophy-of-language']},
  {id: 'reception', title: 'Rivals, decline, and continuing questions', paragraphs: [
    p('mozi-reception-1', 'Confucian thinkers were enduring interlocutors. Mohists criticized costly ritual, graded concern, appeal to inherited custom, and appointments tied to rank; Mencian and Xunzian texts in turn attacked Mohist austerity and impartiality. These exchanges helped define classical Chinese ethics. After political unification, Mohism ceased to survive as an organized school, but explanations for decline are multiple: altered military conditions, absorption of proposals by rivals, hostility to its severe cultural program, and corruption of technical texts all matter. Disappearance as an institution did not mean that every Mohist concept vanished from Chinese thought.', [c('mozi-sep', 'section', '9. Historical Influence and Decline'), c('mozi-iep', 'section', '1. Historical Background'), c('mozi-ethics-sep', 'section', '3. Mohist Ethics')]),
    p('mozi-reception-2', 'Mozi remains philosophically productive when comparisons preserve difference. His ethics anticipates forms of consequential and impartial reasoning, but joins plural social goods to Heaven and centralized order. His merit proposals challenge pedigree without establishing popular sovereignty. His opposition to aggression coexists with coercive government and defensive technology. His appeal to testimony and models invites epistemological reconstruction without collapsing into a modern scientific method. Contemporary terms can help formulate questions today, but they should arrive after the textual distinctions: “utility” must not erase plural goods, “love” must not replace practical concern, and “logic” must not obscure later Mohist analogy and kind-extension. These tensions are not defects to edit away. They show a movement trying to make judgment public, benefit inclusive, and power answerable while relying on institutions whose own dangers it only partly recognizes.', [c('mozi-sep', 'section', '3–9'), c('mozi-ethics-sep', 'section', '3. Mohist Ethics'), c('mozi-iep', 'section', '4. The Aims and Character of Mohist Doctrine')]),
  ]},
  {id: 'reading', title: 'A reading path through layers', paragraphs: [
    p('mozi-reading-1', 'Begin with a specialist overview and a map of the anthology before reading “Mozi” as one voice. In Johnston’s complete translation, compare all three Inclusive Care chapters and all three Against Offensive Warfare chapters, noting changes in arguments and examples. Then read Heaven’s Intention and Elucidating Ghosts alongside them so the religious grounds are visible. Add Elevating the Worthy and Conforming Upward to test whether impartial benefit constrains hierarchy adequately. This sequence reveals a connected program without pretending that every chapter shares one date, author, or perfectly consistent theory.', [c('mozi-sep', 'section', 'Supplement: Texts and Authorship; 2. The Ten Mohist Doctrines'), c('mozi-johnston', 'chapter', '8–19; 26–31'), c('mozi-iep', 'section', '2–3')]),
    p('mozi-reading-2', 'Approach the Canons only after the core vocabulary is familiar, using Fraser’s specialist guide because translation depends on difficult reconstruction. Track who is speaking, which corpus layer a claim belongs to, and whether “love,” “utility,” “law,” or “logic” imports assumptions absent from the Chinese term. A strong final comparison places Mohist care beside Confucian differentiated relationships while asking a concrete question: which goods count, whose loss is visible, and what institution can correct partial judgment? The result should be neither celebration of a modern precursor nor dismissal of an austere ancient program.', [c('mozi-canons-sep', 'section', '1–2; 7. Argumentation and Logic'), c('mozi-ethics-sep', 'section', '3. Mohist Ethics'), c('mozi-iep', 'section', '13. References and Further Reading')]),
  ]},
];

